---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';
---

<Base
  title="Privacy — Volodymyr Pavlyshyn"
  description="What this website collects: nothing. The short version and the long version.">

  <div class="page narrow">
    <header class="head">
      <h1 class="display">Privacy</h1>
      <p class="standfirst">
        The short version: this site sets no cookies, runs no analytics, embeds nothing,
        and collects nothing about you. The long version follows, because GDPR asks for
        the long version.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section>
      <h2 class="section-title">What runs on this site</h2>
      <p>
        No analytics. No tracking pixels. No cookies — not functional ones, not any
        others. No embedded videos, newsletter widgets, comment systems, or social
        buttons. No consent banner, because there is nothing to consent to.
      </p>
      <p>
        Typefaces are served from this site's own server, not from Google Fonts or any
        other font CDN, so loading a page does not disclose your IP address to a third
        party.
      </p>

      <h2 class="section-title">Hosting</h2>
      <p>
        The site is hosted on GitHub Pages (GitHub, Inc., 88 Colin P. Kelly Jr. Street,
        San Francisco, CA 94107, USA). Serving a page necessarily involves GitHub
        processing your IP address and request metadata in their server logs — this is
        technically unavoidable for any website and is the legal basis of Art. 6(1)(f)
        GDPR, legitimate interest in operating the site. I have no access to those logs.
        GitHub's privacy statement:
        {' '}<a href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-privacy-statement">
          docs.github.com
        </a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="section-title">Links away from this site</h2>
      <p>
        Buying a book, installing a tool, following a newsletter or watching a video all
        happen somewhere else — Leanpub, the Visual Studio Marketplace, GitHub, Substack,
        YouTube, Medium, Leaflet. Once you follow such a link, that provider's privacy
        policy applies and this one stops. Nothing is loaded from those services while
        you are on this site; they are ordinary links.
      </p>

      <h2 class="section-title">If you write to me</h2>
      <p>
        If you email <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>, I process what you
        send in order to reply to you (Art. 6(1)(b) and (f) GDPR). I keep correspondence
        as long as it is useful for the enquiry or as long as commercial retention
        obligations require, and I do not add you to any mailing list.
      </p>
      {site.booking && (
        <p>
          If you book a call through the scheduling link, that booking is handled by the
          scheduling provider, who processes the name, email address and times you give
          them in order to make the appointment.
        </p>
      )}

      <h2 class="section-title">Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        You have the right to access, rectification, erasure, restriction of processing,
        and data portability, and the right to object, under Art. 15–21 GDPR. You may
        also complain to a supervisory authority — in Berlin, the Berliner Beauftragte
        für Datenschutz und Informationsfreiheit. To exercise any of these, write to the
        address above.
      </p>

      <h2 class="section-title">Controller</h2>
      <p>
        Volodymyr Pavlyshyn, Berlin, Germany. Full details in the
        {' '}<a href="/impressum">Impressum</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="back"><a href="/">← Back to the front page</a></p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .head { margin: 3.4rem 0 2rem; }
  .section-title { margin-top: 2.2rem; }
  .back { margin-top: 3rem; font-size: 0.9rem; }
</style>
